+# Carrier Crew, Automation, Stations, Damage, and Graceful Degradation Runtime
+
+## Runtime Purpose
+
+Make enormous carriers playable by one person, a cooperative group or a mostly NPC community without pretending that a single captain manually operates a city-sized machine.
+
+The runtime must preserve:
+
+- embodied station play;
+- professional mastery;
+- delegated routine;
+- autonomous crew agency;
+- understandable failure;
+- meaningful command decisions;
+- continuity when players are absent.
+
+# 1. Operational Layers
+
+## 1.1 Local physical work
+
+Inspection, repair, medicine, cargo handling, EVA and machinery operation occur in the physical world.
+
+## 1.2 Station operation
+
+A qualified operator controls one bounded system through instruments, procedures and automation.
+
+## 1.3 Watch coordination
+
+A watch lead coordinates several stations during a period of operation.
+
+## 1.4 Department management
+
+A department allocates labor, maintenance, reserves, training and priorities.
+
+## 1.5 Mission command
+
+Command resolves cross-department tradeoffs and external commitments.
+
+## 1.6 Governance
+
+The carrier polity determines charter, leadership, budgets and rights.
+
+These layers must remain distinct in interface and authority.
+
+# 2. Core Operational Departments
+
+Large carriers need overlapping departments rather than one bridge doing everything.
+
+## Navigation and flight
+
+- trajectory;
+- attitude;
+- traffic;
+- local maneuvering;
+- route planning;
+- docking coordination.
+
+## Engineering
+
+- propulsion;
+- power;
+- thermal;
+- structure;
+- atmosphere and water;
+- damage control.
+
+## Harbor and deployment
+
+- docks;
+- cargo transfer;
+- carried craft;
+- tugs;
+- landing and recovery;
+- quarantine interface.
+
+## Habitat and ecology
+
+- homes;
+- food;
+- water;
+- waste;
+- gardens;
+- public spaces;
+- population needs.
+
+## Care
+
+- medicine;
+- mental health;
+- disability support;
+- quarantine;
+- casualty planning.
+
+## Fabrication and stores
+
+- production;
+- maintenance planning;
+- tools;
+- spares;
+- material accounting.
+
+## Information and contact
+
+- sensors;
+- communications;
+- records;
+- translation;
+- cybersecurity;
+- scientific observation.
+
+## Safety and security
+
+- emergency coordination;
+- fire and pressure response;
+- rescue;
+- proportionate defense;
+- investigation.
+
+## Civic and cultural services
+
+- education;
+- deliberation;
+- justice;
+- archives;
+- art;
+- ritual;
+- hospitality.
+
+Departments can be organized differently by culture, but the work still exists.
+
+# 3. Station Design
+
+A station should expose:
+
+- current state;
+- intended state;
+- uncertainty;
+- constraints;
+- alerts with provenance;
+- available actions;
+- predicted consequences;
+- authority scope;
+- automation state;
+- nearby collaborators;
+- physical location of relevant equipment.
+
+Avoid generic glowing panels disconnected from machinery.
+
+A player should be able to leave the station and inspect the system it represents.
+
+# 4. Qualification and Mastery
+
+Operator capability derives from:
+
+- conceptual knowledge;
+- practiced procedures;
+- familiarity with this carrier;
+- embodiment and tools;
+- current stress and injury;
+- team coordination;
+- automation support;
+- valid authorization.
+
+Progression unlocks better perception, planning and execution—not arbitrary permission to violate physics.
+
+Experienced operators notice:
+
+- subtle trends;
+- misleading sensor combinations;
+- dangerous shortcuts;
+- hidden dependencies;
+- when automation is technically correct but contextually wrong.
+
+# 5. Automation Modes
+
+## Advisory
+
+Automation predicts and recommends; people act.
+
+## Supervised control
+
+Automation operates within a narrow envelope while a qualified person monitors.
+
+## Routine autonomy
+
+Automation performs stable, certified tasks and escalates exceptions.
+
+## Emergency autonomy
+
+Automation acts to preserve life under declared rules when human response is unavailable.
+
+## Delegated mission autonomy
+
+An authorized machine person or agent coordinates a bounded mission with audit and appeal.
+
+Automation state must be visible and attributable.
+
+# 6. Single-Player Accessibility
+
+One player should be able to inhabit a large carrier through delegation.
+
+The game should:
+
+- assign qualified NPCs to routine stations;
+- summarize stable watches;
+- surface meaningful exceptions;
+- let the player choose focus;
+- permit time acceleration during stable operation;
+- provide command recommendations;
+- allow switching to another character when appropriate;
+- preserve consequences of staffing and trust.
+
+The player is not required to perform every watch, but their organization choices matter.
+
+# 7. Co-op Operations
+
+Co-op becomes valuable during:
+
+- difficult docking;
+- high-acceleration burns;
+- storm or atmospheric interface;
+- simultaneous deployments;
+- major damage;
+- quarantine;
+- rescue;
+- combat;
+- first contact;
+- political crisis.
+
+Players may occupy different stations, move physically between them, or command independent carried bases.
+
+No station should consist of passive waiting. Stable tasks can be delegated or compressed.
+
+# 8. Watchstanding
+
+Carrier life uses watches, shifts or culturally equivalent systems.
+
+Watch state includes:
+
+- assigned people;
+- qualifications;
+- fatigue;
+- handover notes;
+- outstanding faults;
+- current mission phase;
+- authority transfer;
+- absence and substitution.
+
+Poor handover can create risk, but should not become repetitive paperwork after mastery.
+
+# 9. Crew Trust and Professional Ethics
+
+Crew response depends on:
+
+- player competence;
+- honesty;
+- treatment of subordinates;
+- respect for safety;
+- reliability;
+- mission values;
+- political legitimacy;
+- past outcomes.
+
+A respected commander can obtain discretionary effort. An abusive commander may face concealment, departure, refusal, union action or lawful removal.
+
+# 10. Damage as Network State
+
+Damage affects component and district graphs.
+
+Damage types include:
+
+- structural fracture;
+- pressure breach;
+- fire;
+- electrical fault;
+- thermal-loop loss;
+- contamination;
+- software compromise;
+- sensor degradation;
+- drive damage;
+- dock obstruction;
+- habitat ecology collapse;
+- personnel loss;
+- maintenance backlog.
+
+# 11. Graceful Degradation
+
+A carrier should often remain alive after losing peak capability.
+
+Possible responses:
+
+- isolate one district;
+- reduce acceleration;
+- shut down a fabrication line;
+- ration heat or water;
+- convert public halls to shelter;
+- use carried craft as auxiliary power or evacuation;
+- split the carrier cluster;
+- abandon a damaged module;
+- accept tow;
+- become a temporary station;
+- change mission.
+
+Failure is a new operating state, not always an explosion.
+
+# 12. Emergency Priorities
+
+Emergency plans contain declared priorities such as:
+
+1. preserve immediate life;
+2. contain cascading hazard;
+3. maintain minimum command and communication;
+4. preserve evacuation paths;
+5. protect care and dependent populations;
+6. retain maneuvering or safe orbit;
+7. preserve irreplaceable ecological and historical continuity;
+8. restore mission capability.
+
+Different charters may order some priorities differently, but cannot erase basic rights.
+
+# 13. Damage-Control Gameplay
+
+Meaningful tasks include:
+
+- diagnose source rather than chase alarms;
+- close and verify boundaries;
+- reroute power and fluids;
+- physically reach damaged equipment;
+- coordinate suits and tools;
+- move residents;
+- choose repair versus isolation;
+- use carried craft;
+- fabricate substitute parts;
+- manage heat and time;
+- document uncertainty.
+
+After familiar procedures are mastered, routine steps compress while diagnosis and decisions remain.
+
+# 14. Command Loss and Succession
+
+The carrier must continue if the bridge, captain or player is unavailable.
+
+Succession state includes:
+
+- declared chain;
+- department autonomy;
+- emergency council;
+- machine authority;
+- external trustees;
+- quorum rules;
+- later review.
+
+Command loss should create tension without making the entire ship stupid.
+
+# 15. Enemy and Horror Interactions
+
+The director may exploit actual vulnerabilities but cannot cheat.
+
+Threats may:
+
+- observe emissions;
+- damage exposed systems;
+- infiltrate through valid access;
+- disrupt communications;
+- exploit neglected maintenance;
+- target docks or logistics;
+- spread contamination through real paths.
+
+They may not:
+
+- spawn in sealed spaces;
+- know private plans without an information path;
+- force a failure because pacing is low;
+- disable redundant systems without affecting them.
+
+# 16. Player Legibility
+
+At every scale, players need to know:
+
+- what is wrong;
+- what is believed rather than confirmed;
+- who is working on it;
+- what happens if ignored;
+- what resources are needed;
+- what authority they possess;
+- what tradeoffs an action creates.
+
+Use map layers, physical signs, crew reports, IRIS interpretation and station instruments. Avoid one omniscient damage list unless accessibility settings request it.
+
+# 17. Performance Model
+
+Distant stable systems update in coarse intervals.
+
+High-frequency simulation is reserved for:
+
+- active maneuvers;
+- damaged networks;
+- player-local machinery;
+- life-critical thresholds;
+- combat and docking;
+- time-sensitive ecology.
+
+Named crew and continuity-critical institutions persist even when their routine work is aggregated.
+
+# 18. First Benchmark Scenario
+
+One multirole carrier performs:
+
+- routine transit;
+- crawler deployment;
+- an external craft return with contamination uncertainty;
+- simultaneous radiator damage;
+- a clinic surge;
+- command-player disconnection;
+- NPC succession;
+- partial district isolation;
+- recovery without total mission failure.
+
+The scenario must be completable in single-player assisted mode and four-player professional mode from the same authoritative state.
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **The ship becomes playable not when one person can do everything, but when responsibility can move between people without the civilization losing itself.**
